{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779230282,"nonce":"RWs93UtCfOQKG4ZRRZVefNVGtQU=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCx92hw","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/140","origin":"github"},"title":"Remove stale bencloud ping target from operator-side ping_monitor (doesn't reflect WG link health)","message":"## Problem\n\n`/Other/ping_monitor: ping.op: bencloud` shows **Unreachable (100% packet loss)** in `/diagnostics_agg` on the operator station (salmon log §10 from the [2026-05-19 deployment](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/135)). This drives the toplevel diagnostics summary to ERROR even when nothing operationally is wrong.\n\n## Background\n\nThe target was added as an attempt to monitor the operator-side WireGuard link by pinging bencloud's WireGuard address. **That doesn't work** — pinging bencloud's WireGuard address from operator-side returns 100% loss as a normal condition, so the monitor was never going to reflect link health correctly.\n\n(Bare `ping bencloud` from the shell does resolve and reach a different address, but that's not the WG link being checked.)\n\n## Fix\n\nRemove the `bencloud` target from the operator-side `ping_monitor` config. The right place is the operator launch file / config that feeds `ping_monitor`'s target list — likely under `bizzyboat_project11/launch/network_monitor_operator_launch.py` or whatever YAML it loads.\n\nIf a working operator-side WireGuard link check is still wanted, that's a separate piece of work (different mechanism — e.g., WG handshake age from `wg show`, or pinging a host on the other side of the WG tunnel that does respond).\n\nPart of #135\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}